; Exercise 4.14.  Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner are each experimenting with the metacircular evaluator. Eva types in the definition of map, and runs some test programs that use it. They work fine. Louis, in contrast, has installed the system version of map as a primitive for the metacircular evaluator. When he tries it, things go terribly wrong. Explain why Louis's map fails even though Eva's works.

; Eva's version is native to metacircular evaluator, so it uses eval and apply and almost everything else from metacircular evaluator, so everything works. This version uses functions from original interpreter only when it is explicitly allowed - when they're added as a primitives to metacircular evaluator.

; Louis installed system version inside the metacircular evaluator. We don't know how exactly this map function is implemented, but it easily could be implemented much different from our approach. Well, it may be even be implemented as a primitive on some low-level language. So yes, it may not work when we pass it our procedures defined inside the metacircular evaluator, which original map probably knows nothing about. In fact, it would be almost impossible luck if it worked as expected. It would require a map to be implemented in such a way that executing it will use functions that we redefined inside out metacircular evaluator, not the original functions from the interpreter.
